vLLM 0.34 turns on the extensible KV cache by default on CUDA: it reserves the pool's address range and commits
physical pages only after warmup and CUDA-graph capture. KVarN's batched flush writes the KV pool during capture,
so every KVarN profile without a --kv-cache-memory pin (SPEC=off CTX=huge, SPEC=mtp CTX=huge, batch KV=kvarn)
died in capture with an illegal memory access in kvarn_attn _batched_flush, past the 0.03 GiB the cache had
committed by then (threadchip's S2 boots on an RTX 3090, 11-19). With --disable-extensible-kv-cache all three boot
and serve, and their outputs match 0.33's. The pinned DFlash2 CTX=huge profile was never affected, since a
kv_cache_memory_bytes pin already turns the cache off.

So the resolver treats a KVarN cache dtype as unsupported, beside kv_cache_memory_bytes and
num_gpu_blocks_override. Unset, it resolves off and logs why. An explicit --enable-extensible-kv-cache is refused
with the reason. Every other cache dtype resolves as before.

Kind: own
What: KVarN cache dtypes turn the extensible KV cache off (its flush writes the pool during CUDA-graph capture,
  before the cache commits pages), so unpinned KVarN profiles boot on 0.34
Upstream: none
Cut-against: 0.34.0
Retires-when: KVarN commits the blocks its capture-time flush addresses (ensure_kv_cache_blocks), or rides with KVarN

